\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces Size comparison of various particles. Nanoparticles are defined as particles where the largest dimension is at most ${\sim }\SI {200}{\nm }$. Figures taken from "Comparison of nanomaterials sizes" by Sureshbup, available under CC BY-SA 3.0 at \href {https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comparison_of_nanomaterials_sizes.jpg}{URL}}}{2}{figure.caption.10}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Linear-superposition mobility curve for linear-superposition and Faxén relations with $h = \SI {225}{\nm }$.}}{16}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces The y-axis plots the right-hand side of Eqn. \ref {eqn:selfconsist} for the self-consistent diffusion estimation in confinement. An $a=\sim \SI {50}{\nm }$ polystyrene bead was imaged and tracked in the transverse plane as provided by the manufacturer. The initial size estimate before confinement analysis was $\hat {a} = \SI {124.30}{\nm }$. Post self-consistency, applying a linear superposition theory estimate of the confinement effects, $a^* = \SI {64.1}{\nm }$. }}{16}{figure.caption.12}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Lipid nanoparticle diagram and CRYO-EM image. CRYO-EM image obtained from the NIST Segmentation of Lipid Nanoparticles \href {https://data.nist.gov/od/id/mds2-2753}{dataset} \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {NIST2023}. Shown are KC2 LNPs at pH 7, with RNA taken at $\SI {0.223}{\nm }$ per pixel.}}{23}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces mRNA-LNP types diagram. Black is lipids, grey is hydrophobic region, red is mRNA, orange is oil-core. Taken from Ref. \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {Simonsen2024} under a creative-commons license 4.0}}{24}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Locating encapsulated mRNA in LNPs by CRYO-EM. The effect of thionine on mRNA-LNPs of different morphologies reveals that lipid-dissociated mRNA may reside in bleb compartments. (a) or may be more lipid-associated in spherical (b) or less prominently blebbed particles (c). (d) Charge-driven migration of mRNA from the bleb into the body of the LNP. Modified from Ref. \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {brader_encapsulation_2021} under a creative-commons license 4.0.}}{25}{figure.caption.15}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Rayleigh scattering pattern, Eqn. \ref {eqn:rayleigh_scat}, plotted for various sized particles. The $y$-axis, plotted in units of optical intensity, denotes the amplitude of the modulus of the scattered field. The $x$-axis plots the distance from the center of the scatterer in $\mathrm {nm}$.Parameters fixed such that $\lambda = \SI {405}{\nm }, n_m = 1.33, E_0 = 1, \theta = 0$. Particle parameters set as $n_p = 1.59, \alpha \propto V$. As the particle volume increases, the scattering pattern increases in amplitude.}}{32}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Simple imaging setup. Left, a lens is placed before an object. The light focuses on an imaging plane. The schematic consists of a feasible setup. Right, an image consists of a plane wave illumination, a lens, and a camera.}}{38}{figure.caption.17}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Isometric iSCAT Schematic. A laser is first shaped using a spatial filter for a gaussian beam profile. The gaussian beam is sized by an aperture and focused into the back-plane of an objective. During the focusing, the light is circularly polarized. At the sample plane, two coverslips are placed for sample containment. To control the gap between the two coverslips, the convex-lens induced confinement (CLiC) system is used. The scattered and reflected light is collected through a polarizing beam-splitter and the final image is focused on a CMOS camera array.}}{41}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Optical table for the iSCAT microscope. Photographed at the Leslie lab, UBC.}}{43}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces Optical table for the iSCAT microscope. Photographed at the Leslie lab, UBC.}}{43}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces Laser Driver for the $\SI {405}{\nm }$ laser diode. }}{44}{figure.caption.22}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Model diagram of simulation geometry. A spherical particle of $\zeta $-potential $\SI {25}{\milli \volt 4}$ and radius $a=\SI {50}{\nm }$ is modeled between two charged parallel plates spaced by $L = \SI {300}{\nm }$. The electrostatic potential, modeled by the Debye-Hückel potential, are drawn in a blue gradient and fixed at a Debye-length of $\SI {40}{\nm }$. The values are chosen to model a semi-realistic system, such as a plastic bead in a salt solution while still providing an artistic rendering for intuition.}}{50}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Langevin simulation function \textsc {StepTraj} time complexity. Simulation parameters are set as default with $dt = 2\cdot 10^{-7}$. The number of simultaneous particles is $1$, and the number of recorded positions consists of all of the points in the trajectory. In big-O notation, the time complexity is $\mathcal {O}\left (n^1\right )$. Simulations performed on a 2020 MacBook Pro with ARM M1 processor.}}{53}{figure.caption.27}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.3}{\ignorespaces Langevin simulation function \textsc {StepTraj} time complexity. Simulation parameters are set as Fig. \ref {fig:timecomplex1} with two parallel simulations.}}{53}{figure.caption.28}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.4}{\ignorespaces Confined Brownian Particle realization of $z$ diffusion in electrostatic potential. The step-histogram is plotted in green. Simulation input parameters are $a = \SI {50}{\nm }, \zeta _p = \SI {-3.5}{\milli \volt }, \sigma _w = \SI {-2}{\milli \coulomb / \meter ^2}, L = \SI {225}{\nm }, \lambda _D = \SI {100}{\nm }, T = \SI {300}{\kelvin }, \eta = \SI {1}{\milli \pascal \second }$.}}{54}{figure.caption.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.5}{\ignorespaces Gibbs-Boltzmann equilibrium of a confined Brownian particle. The green histogram is the experimentally measured distribution while the blue line is the theoretical distribution. Simulation input parameters are $a = \SI {50}{\nm }, \zeta _p = \SI {-3.5}{\milli \volt }, \sigma _w = \SI {-2}{\milli \coulomb / \meter ^2}, L = \SI {225}{\nm }, \lambda _D = \SI {100}{\nm }, T = \SI {300}{\kelvin }, \eta = \SI {1}{\milli \pascal \second }$.}}{55}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.6}{\ignorespaces Comparison between MSD curves of two different charged particles. The green trace represents a neutrally charged particle with a maximal MSD of $\sim \SI {26000}{\nm ^2}$. The blue trace represents a negatively charged particle with a maximal MSD of $\sim \SI {15000}{\nm ^2}$. The chart on the right shows the transverse MSD of the two particles. Each unit of $\tau $ consists of on exposure time of $\SI {200}{\micro \second }$. The Debye-length was fixed to $\lambda _D = \SI {10}{\nm }$. }}{56}{figure.caption.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.7}{\ignorespaces Effective confinement length $L$, or the minimum and maximum trajectory axial position, plotted (left) against the solution's Debye-length $\lambda _D$ and (right) expressed in terms of ion concentration $C$. The blue dotted line represents the confinement lengths inferred from the average plateau values of 1000 simulated confined diffusions. The green dashed line represents the theoretical $L-2a$ confinement length, which results from the total length subtracted by the diameter of the particle. The particle parameters were chosen to be $a = \SI {50}{\nm }, \zeta = \SI {-30}{\milli \volt }$. The geometric conditions are: $\sigma _w = \SI {-2}{\milli \coulomb / \meter ^2}, L = \SI {500}{\nm }, T = \SI {300}{\kelvin }, \Delta t_i = \SI {0.2}{\micro \second }, \tau = \SI {200}{\micro \second }, \eta = \SI {1}{\milli \pascal \second }$.}}{57}{figure.caption.32}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.8}{\ignorespaces Effective diffusion $D/D_0$, or the measure diffusion coefficient compared to the input diffusion, plotted against the solution's Debye length expressed as concentration $C$ of ions. The red dashed line represents the equality that the diffusion coefficient measured is the same as that input into the simulation. The blue dotted lines represent the ratio of diffusion coefficients measured using line-fitting of the first 3 points of the $\mathrm {MSD}_x$. The particle parameters were chosen to be $a = \SI {50}{\nm }, \zeta = \SI {-30}{\milli \volt }$. The geometric conditions are: $\sigma _w = \SI {-2}{\milli \coulomb / \meter ^2}, L = \SI {500}{\nm }, T = \SI {300}{\kelvin }, \Delta t_i = \SI {0.2}{\micro \second }, \tau = \SI {200}{\micro \second }, \eta = \SI {1}{\milli \pascal \second }$.}}{58}{figure.caption.33}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.9}{\ignorespaces Effective confinement plotted against the particle's zeta-potential. Each curve represents one Debye-length labelled on the right-hand side. The zeta-potential of the particle is varied on the $x$-axis, while the recovered confinement length is plotted on the $y$-axis. Simulation parameters are identical to that of Fig. \ref {fig:conf_debyelen}.}}{59}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.10}{\ignorespaces Distribution of $\hat {B}$ Re-parameterized for the surface charge. $\hat {\sigma } = 1/(A\cdot C)^2 \hat {B}$ where $A,C$ are properly defined in Eqn. \ref {eqn:amp1} and Eqn. \ref {eqn:amp2} .}}{61}{figure.caption.35}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.1}{\ignorespaces Outline of the 3D particle-tracking pipeline.}}{63}{figure.caption.36}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.2}{\ignorespaces Raw image captured on camera to background subtracted iSCAT image. Single particles are tracked and extracted for further analysis.}}{64}{figure.caption.37}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.3}{\ignorespaces Raw image to RVT. Gaussian fitting of the RVT is shown on both plots.}}{65}{figure.caption.38}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.4}{\ignorespaces Demonstration of the Radial Symmetry method applied onto RVT of iSCAT data. Implemented from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {parthasarathyRapidAccurateParticle2012}. Figure adapted from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {parthasarathyRapidAccurateParticle2012}. }}{66}{figure.caption.39}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.5}{\ignorespaces Angular Integral demonstration on experimental polystyrene image. The polar distance, $\rho $ is plotted here where the red $\times $ symbol denotes the center of the image.}}{67}{figure.caption.40}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.7}{\ignorespaces Simulated iPSF. Left. Projection of in-focus plane. Middle. $x,z$ projection. Right. $y,z$ projection. Focal scan is over $\SI {400}{\nm }$. pixel size is $\SI {55}{\nm }/\text {pixel}$. $\lambda = \SI {405}{\nm }$. Assumed particle radius $a = \SI {50}{\nm }$.}}{70}{figure.caption.42}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.6}{\ignorespaces Viterbi Search algorithm toy example. Optimal path is chosen based on $z$ similarity and lowest loss.}}{70}{figure.caption.41}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.8}{\ignorespaces Nuisance Parameter estimation for iPSF fits. Left. Amplitude factor. right. exponential decay factor. The exponential decay is not of utmost importance as it often is very small. it is kept for stability purposes.}}{71}{figure.caption.43}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.9}{\ignorespaces Fit residuals using no weighting scheme. reporting an absolute bias of 1 nm with variance of 2 nanometers.}}{72}{figure.caption.44}%
